WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.260
A barley leaf cannot take in CO2 without letting water out.

2
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.380
The gas enters through tiny pores called stomata.

3
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.720
Water vapour leaves through the very same pores.

4
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.300
When the stomata are open, both things happen at once.

5
00:00:18.283 --> 00:00:21.703
An adult human lung faces a version of the same bargain.

6
00:00:22.703 --> 00:00:26.743
Gas must dissolve in a thin, wet film before it can diffuse into cells.

7
00:00:27.643 --> 00:00:30.443
A wet surface in air loses water by evaporation.

8
00:00:31.423 --> 00:00:34.743
In ordinary, drier air, each breath carries water away.

9
00:00:35.643 --> 00:00:36.983
A stoma can close its gap.

10
00:00:36.983 --> 00:00:40.263
The lung sits inside the chest and cannot close.

11
00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:42.613
How do we count the cost?

12
00:00:43.733 --> 00:00:45.573
Divide the carbon gained by the water lost.

13
00:00:46.493 --> 00:00:48.153
This is called water-use efficiency.

14
00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:51.513
Here are two made-up leaves, not barley data.

15
00:00:52.493 --> 00:00:58.913
Leaf A gains 20 µmol CO2 and loses 5 mmol H2O, per m2 per s.

16
00:00:59.813 --> 00:01:04.613
Its efficiency is 4.0 µmol CO2 per mmol H2O.

17
00:01:05.617 --> 00:01:07.357
Leaf B has fewer stomata.

18
00:01:08.257 --> 00:01:12.217
It gains sixteen and loses three, so its efficiency is five point three.

19
00:01:13.037 --> 00:01:17.097
It gains twenty percent less carbon, but loses forty percent less water.

20
00:01:18.097 --> 00:01:19.557
Neither leaf is better everywhere.

21
00:01:20.537 --> 00:01:24.397
When water is scarce, leaf B keeps more water for each unit of carbon.

22
00:01:25.217 --> 00:01:29.217
When water is plentiful and carbon limits growth, leaf A gains more.

23
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.260
You might expect less grain from a leaf with fewer stomata.

24
00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:35.740
Researchers tested this in barley.

25
00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:41.260
They made one barley gene, which limits stomatal development, produce more of its

26
00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:42.260
product than usual.

27
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.710
In the mature sixth leaf, two lines kept about twenty-four percent and twelve

28
00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:49.760
percent of the control stomatal density.

29
00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.600
Gas exchange fell in well-watered plants.

30
00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:58.000
With five plants per group, no statistically significant drop in grain yield was

31
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.700
seen. That does not prove the yields equal.

32
00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.140
It means no loss was detected.

33
00:02:04.183 --> 00:02:06.763
Why does a structure usually fit what it does?

34
00:02:08.003 --> 00:02:09.623
Selection acts on inherited variation.

35
00:02:10.623 --> 00:02:15.623
A sound claim is that lineages with fewer stomata lost less water and left more

36
00:02:15.623 --> 00:02:17.083
descendants in dry places.

37
00:02:18.243 --> 00:02:21.183
Saying barley has fewer stomata to save water is shorthand.

38
00:02:22.163 --> 00:02:25.503
It hides that history, and can sound as if the plant had a goal.

39
00:02:26.403 --> 00:02:29.723
And the fit is never perfect, because a trade-off links two traits.

40
00:02:30.643 --> 00:02:32.163
Improving one worsens the other.

41
00:02:32.163 --> 00:02:34.883
Every exchange surface carries a cost.
